    <button x="220" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="95" display="go once">go</button>
    <monitor x="455" precision="0" y="65" height="60" fontSize="12" width="130" display="Population">count turtles</monitor>
    <plot x="455" autoPlotX="true" yMax="100.0" autoPlotY="true" yAxis="Agents" y="140" xMin="0.0" height="350" legend="true" xMax="100.0" yMin="0.0" width="510" xAxis="Ticks" display="Population by strategy">
      <setup/>
      <update/>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Humanitarian" color="-16777216" legend="true">
        <setup/>
        <update>plot humanitarian-count</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Ethnocentric" color="-2674135" legend="true">
        <setup/>
        <update>plot ethnocentric-count</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Egoist" color="-13345367" legend="true">
        <setup/>
        <update>plot egoist-count</update>
      </pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Traitorous" color="-10899396" legend="true">
        <setup/>
        <update>plot traitorous-count</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## O_6: Immigration

### Purpose: adding newcomers

O_6 adds immigration to the revised O_5. One newcomer enters a randomly selected empty patch at the start of each tick, if space exists. It receives an independently random tag and profile and starts at score zero.

The other rules remain: deterministic profile-based PD choices, per-tick score reset, local score-dependent birth, independent mutation at birth, and random death.

### How to use

Click setup, then go or go once. Setup creates 100 agents on distinct patches in a 33 by 33 wrapping world. The run ends at 2000 ticks, as specified directly in go.

Unlike O_5, extinction does not stop the run: immigration can restart an empty population. A fully occupied world skips that tick's arrival attempt.

### Agent states

- score: current-tick PD payoff, reset before games.
- played?: current-tick participation record; it does not limit games.
- original-color: display color for the group.
- group-tag: observable group marker, 0 to 3.
- profile: humanitarian, ethnocentric, egoist, or traitorous.

Neighbors and cooperation flags are computed locally rather than stored. Existing agents stay fixed. Only newcomers and offspring receive new locations.

### Four profiles

| Profile | Same group | Different group | Shape |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Humanitarian | Cooperate | Cooperate | Circle |
| Ethnocentric | Cooperate | Defect | Square |
| Egoist | Defect | Defect | Triangle |
| Traitorous | Defect | Cooperate | X |

Tags do not determine profiles. Any profile can occur in any group. The model specifies the profiles; their prevalence is an outcome to investigate.

### Procedures and schedule

setup clears the world, colors patches white, calls populate, and initializes CLOCK. populate and immigrate both use initialize-agent to assign a random tag and profile, score zero, and appearance.

go follows this schedule:
1. Stop if ticks >= 2000.
2. immigrate: add one newcomer if an empty patch exists.
3. reset-scores: set current agents' scores to zero.
4. clear-participation: restore group colors and clear played?.
5. Select agents with count turtles-on neighbors4 > 0 and ask them to play.
6. Ask agents present at the start of the birth stage to reproduce.
7. Ask the current population, including newborns and the immigrant, to face-death.
8. tick advances CLOCK and updates the population plot.

A newcomer can play, reproduce, or die during its arrival tick. An offspring cannot play or reproduce until the next tick, but can die in its birth tick.

### Immigration versus birth

immigrate selects an empty patch anywhere in the world and creates one agent with random traits. No parent is required. It does not run mutate on arrival because its initial traits are already assigned randomly.

reproduce requires a living parent, a successful birth attempt, and an empty cardinally adjacent patch. The offspring inherits its parent's traits, then undergoes mutation. Immigrants' future offspring follow exactly this same rule.

### Playing and payoffs

Each occupied neighbors4 pair plays once per tick. The higher-who comparison prevents duplicate games, and each game updates both players. All occupied cardinal neighbors count, including across wrapping boundaries. Diagonals and the same patch are excluded. Isolates skip games but can reproduce and die.

action-toward compares group tags and applies each player's profile, returning 0 for cooperate or 1 for defect. Cooperation choices are not random and use no memory or learning.

pd-payoff reports: CC gives 2 each; CD gives -1 to the cooperator and 3 to the defector; DD gives 0 each. Cooperation costs the actor 1 and benefits the recipient by 3.

### Reproduction and death

birth-probability reports 12 + this tick's score, bounded to 0-100 percent. Each existing agent gets one birth attempt. A successful attempt still needs an empty neighbors4 patch. No vacancy means no newborn. Scores are not spent.

hatch copies the parent, places the newborn, resets score and played?, calls mutate, and refreshes appearance. Parents stay in place and retain their traits.

face-death gives every current agent an independent 10% death chance, unrelated to score. Death opens vacancies for later ticks.

### HA-style mutation

Mutation occurs once per offspring at birth, independently of its neighborhood or score.

The procedure temporarily decodes profile into help-same? and help-different?. It performs three independent tests:
- Group tag: 0.5% chance to change to one of the other three tags.
- Same-group behavioral rule: 0.5% chance to flip.
- Different-group behavioral rule: 0.5% chance to flip.

profile-from-rules stores the resulting profile. The temporary flags are not persistent states. update-appearance updates its color and shape. The parent is unaffected.

The tag changes with probability 0.5%. The profile changes with probability 1 - (0.995 * 0.995) = 0.9975%. Neither, either one, or both may change. There is no social-exposure trigger and no 5% mutation rule in this version.

### Appearance and population plot

Colors show groups: blue 0, green 1, orange 2, violet 3. Shapes show profiles. There is no red player or yellow patch highlighting.

Plot colors identify profiles across all groups: Humanitarian black, Ethnocentric red, Egoist blue, Traitorous green. These are independent of agents' group colors.

The four named count reporters feed the line plot; their sum equals count turtles. Scores remain internal to reproduction. Mutation and immigration can reintroduce lost traits, but their rates are different processes and should be compared through experiments.

### Following Hammond–Axelrod progressively

O_6 now combines inherited conditional behavior, local reproduction, per-period payoff reset, unconditional 0.5% per-trait mutation, 10% death, and one immigrant per tick. These mechanisms follow HA. Our initial population of 100 agents and 33 by 33 world remain teaching choices; HA's standard experiment starts empty on a 50 by 50 world.

Source: Hammond and Axelrod (2006), The Evolution of Ethnocentrism, manuscript pp. 3-4 and 8-9: https://websites.umich.edu/~axe/research/Hammond%20and%20Axelrod%20JCR%2006.pdf

### Credits

Professor Jose Manuel MAGALLANES  
Complexity and ABM, 2026  
DACSS - UMASS
]]></info>
